  "image_name": "e6cde0a1b5d12986ba651f45274cb5d1.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure illustrates a progression from ordinary generative modeling to condition-aware generation and then to latent-space optimization. On the left, the model learns a single overall data distribution, shown as a broad green region containing both training samples and generated samples. This represents an unconditional distribution, where the generator tries to reproduce the full spread of observed data without targeting any specific property.\n\nIn the middle, the same idea is refined into conditional distributions, labeled by wavelength values such as 800, 900, and 1,000 nm. Separate colored clusters indicate that the data are organized according to a conditioning variable, so generated samples can be directed toward a desired subset rather than drawn from the whole population. Training and generated points are shown within these property-specific regions, suggesting controlled generation based on the chosen condition.\n\nOn the right, the focus shifts to searching within the model’s latent space. A red blurred region marks the latent space, and a highlighted star indicates an optimal latent variable. This conveys that, instead of only sampling randomly, one can optimize the hidden representation to find designs or outputs expected to best satisfy a target objective. Overall, the figure emphasizes increasingly purposeful data generation.",
